Experimental, not shipped: expose the 840/900 MHz GPU operating points and request full DDR
bandwidth at the top three points on the Radxa Dragon Q6A.

Status (2026-10-06): the board did not boot with this applied (it was deployed together with
the bandwidth change, and the two were never tested apart). It is also not useful for games:
measured GPU busy time while playing stays at 45% median and 63% at most, so the GPU is not the
bottleneck. LDSR also requires clocks that follow the SoC vendor's binning, which this exceeds.
Kept for reference only. Apply in the kernel tree with `git apply`.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/qcs6490-radxa-dragon-q6a.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/qcs6490-radxa-dragon-q6a.dts
@@ -514,6 +514,52 @@
 	firmware-name = "qcom/qcs6490/a660_zap.mbn";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Expose the TURBO and TURBO_L1 GPU operating points, which raises the ceiling from 812 MHz
+ * to 900 MHz.
+ *
+ * kodiak.dtsi marks both as opp-supported-hw = <0x02>, i.e. speed bin 1 only. This board is
+ * bin 2: of the masks in that table (0x17, 0x16, 0x06) the only bit common to all three that
+ * is absent from 0x02 is bit 2, and every OPP carrying it is exposed here while these two are
+ * not. Adding that bit lets devfreq use them; rpmhpd supplies the TURBO and TURBO_L1 corners,
+ * which are ordinary levels present in cmd-db.
+ *
+ * This is above what Qualcomm validated for this bin, so it is a deliberate overclock rather
+ * than a fix: the part was not tested at 900 MHz. Cooling does not decide the outcome, silicon
+ * leakage does. A failure shows up as a GMU fault or rendering artefacts and is undone by
+ * reverting this override.
+ */
+&gpu_opp_table {
+	/*
+	 * Raise the DDR bandwidth request at the top operating points. kodiak.dtsi asks for
+	 * 8532000 kB/s from 700 MHz upwards, the same figure sc7180 uses, while this board
+	 * carries LPDDR5-5500 on a 32-bit bus, which is 22 GB/s. The highest request anywhere
+	 * in the system is the CPU cluster's 12790000 on its own path to EBI1, so the memory
+	 * controller never sees a reason to leave its middle states.
+	 *
+	 * The interconnect aggregates peak requests by taking the maximum, so asking for the
+	 * full bus at the points where the GPU is working keeps DDR in its top state for the
+	 * whole frame rather than ramping per access pattern. This is a deliberately inflated
+	 * demand, not a measurement of what the GPU consumes. It costs memory power and buys
+	 * latency; RPMh clamps anything the hardware cannot provide, so it cannot ask for an
+	 * impossible state. Points below 812 MHz keep their stock request so that light loads
+	 * do not hold DDR high.
+	 */
+	opp-812000000 {
+		opp-peak-kBps = <22000000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-840000000 {
+		opp-supported-hw = <0x06>;
+		opp-peak-kBps = <22000000>;
+	};
+
+	opp-900000000 {
+		opp-supported-hw = <0x06>;
+		opp-peak-kBps = <22000000>;
+	};
+};
+
 /* Pin 13, 15 in GPIO header */
 &i2c0 {
 	qcom,enable-gsi-dma;
